Often, yes—at least in the broad sense that hierarchy works by attaching consequences to noncompliance.

But it helps to separate a few things that all feel like “coercion” from below:

* Authority: “Do X because this role legitimately gives me decision rights here.”
* Incentive: “Do X and you get rewarded; don’t and you lose an opportunity.”
* Constraint: “These are the rules of the system whether either of us likes them or not.”
* Coercion: “Do X or I deliberately impose harm on you to force compliance.”

A boss can fire you, a bank can enforce a contract, a government can fine you, a platform can ban you. Those are all power relationships. Whether they count as coercion depends on legitimacy, consent, proportionality, exit options, and how much genuine choice you had in the first place.

And that is probably the sharper point underneath what you were saying: people with more leverage routinely make their priorities become other people’s priorities. The difference is that durable power usually does it through structure—contracts, incentives, access, deadlines, reputation, institutional authority—not through uncontrolled retaliation.

So if you want the useful version of the pattern: don’t merely ask, “Who is above me?” Ask, “What gives them leverage over me, what gives me leverage over them, and how can I reduce asymmetric dependence?” That is a much cleaner map of power.